Item Response Theory for DICHOTOMOUS Items:
Dichotomous IRT models are best understood through Item Characteristic Curves (ICCs), which illustrate the relationship between a respondent’s latent trait level and the probability of a correct or endorsed response.

x axis: latent trait or ability
y axis: probability of a correct response

Common dichotomous IRT models include:
Rasch model (1PL)
Estimates item difficulty only and is the most restrictive model.

Two Parameter Logistic model (2PL)
Estimates item difficulty and item discrimination.

Three Parameter Logistic model (3PL)
Estimates item difficulty, discrimination, and a guessing parameter.

------------
### R Code
### Rasch_model
### Data MUST be either 0 or 1, representing True/False or Correct/Incorrect. 

library(eRm)
rasch_model <- RM(irt_data)
summary(rasch_model)

plotICC(
  rasch_model,
  item.subset = 1,
  main = "Item Characteristic Curve (Rasch Model)"
)

------------
### R Code
### (2PL)
model_2pl <- mirt(irt_data, 1, itemtype = "2PL")
summary(model_2pl)
coef(model_2pl, simplify = TRUE)
plot(
  model_2pl,
  type = "trace",
  which.items = 1,
  main = "Item Characteristic Curve (2PL Model)"
)

------------
### R Code
### (3PL)

model_3pl <- mirt(
  irt_data,
  1,
  itemtype = "3PL",
  technical = list(NCYCLES = 1000),
  verbose = TRUE
)
summary(model_3pl)
coef(model_3pl, simplify = TRUE)

plot(
  model_3pl,
  type = "trace",
  which.items = 1,
  main = "Item Characteristic Curve (3PL Model)"
)
